   $q$Which sentence uses hyphens correctly?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Odalys found a nineteenth century atlas, and most of its maps were drawn early in the nineteenth century.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"missing-hyphen-before-noun"},{"text":"Odalys found a nineteenth-century atlas, and most of its maps were drawn early in the nineteenth century.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Odalys found a nineteenth-century atlas, and most of its maps were drawn early in the nineteenth-century.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"hyphen-in-noun-phrase"},{"text":"Odalys found a nineteenth century-atlas, and most of its maps were drawn early in the nineteenth century.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"hyphen-joins-noun"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Odalys found a nineteenth-century atlas, and most of its maps were drawn early in the nineteenth century.$q$, $q$"Nineteenth-century" is hyphenated when it modifies the noun "atlas," but "the nineteenth century" at the end is a noun phrase on its own, so it takes no hyphen. Hyphenating both uses ignores the difference between a modifier and a noun.$q$, 'draft', null)
