-- Passage wg11-informative-writing-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9186f267-1868-5c34-9278-a436e57d13e4', 'wg11-informative-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Damaged Codes Still Scan$q$, $q$(1) A square scannable code printed on a poster can often be read even when part of it is torn, smudged or covered by a sticker. (2) This resilience is not luck. (3) It comes from error correction, a technique that adds carefully calculated extra information to a message so that missing or damaged pieces can be rebuilt.

(4) The simplest way to understand error correction is through repetition. (5) Imagine sending the digit seven three times; if noise changes one copy to a one, the receiver can still choose the majority value. (6) Repetition works, but it is wasteful, because it triples the length of every message.

(7) Modern codes use a more efficient approach. (8) Instead of copying the message, they compute additional symbols, called parity symbols, from mathematical relationships among the original data. (9) If some symbols are damaged, the scanner uses those relationships to solve for the missing values, much as a student solves for an unknown in an equation. (10) Square codes typically offer four levels of protection, the strongest of which can recover the message even if about 30 percent of the code is unreadable. (11) Stuff like this is all over the place, like on snack boxes and stuff.

(12) The same principle protects data far beyond posters. (13) Spacecraft use error correction to send images across vast distances, and storage drives use it to repair bits that weaken over time.$q$, 'draft')
