insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a1a40f49-0875-533b-ba78-63e58c753805', 'wg11-paragraph-transitions-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How the Railroads Changed the Clock$q$, $q$(1) For most of history, every town kept its own time, setting its clocks so that noon fell at the moment the sun stood highest overhead. (2) Because the sun reaches that point slightly later as one travels west, two towns only a short distance apart might disagree by several minutes. (3) ____ travel was slow, such differences hardly mattered, since no one could cross the distance quickly enough to notice them.

(4) The arrival of the railroad changed this situation dramatically. (5) A train could now cover in a few hours a distance that had once taken days, and each station along its route might keep a different local time. (6) Printed schedules became bewildering documents, listing arrivals according to dozens of separate local clocks. (7) More seriously, confusion about the time could place two trains on the same single track at once. (8) Many early locomotives burned wood before they were converted to burn coal.

(9) ____, in the late nineteenth century, railroad companies agreed to divide the continent into a few broad time zones, each one hour apart. (10) Within each zone, every clock showed the same time, regardless of the local position of the sun. (11) Some communities initially resisted, objecting that their clocks should follow the sun rather than a railroad timetable. (12) Over the following decades, however, the convenience of a shared standard won out, and the zones eventually gained legal recognition. (13) Today the system is so familiar that few people realize it began as a practical answer to a very specific problem.$q$, 'draft')
