   $q$Which version of the sentence correctly marks the boundary between its independent clauses?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The researchers expected the monarch butterflies to arrive in early October instead the first ones appeared two weeks late, after an unusually warm September.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"fused-sentence"},{"text":"The researchers expected the monarch butterflies to arrive in early October, instead, the first ones appeared two weeks late, after an unusually warm September.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-with-conjunctive-adverb"},{"text":"The researchers expected the monarch butterflies to arrive in early October; instead, the first ones appeared two weeks late, after an unusually warm September.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The researchers expected the monarch butterflies to arrive in early October; instead, the first ones appeared two weeks late; after an unusually warm September.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-before-phrase"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The researchers expected the monarch butterflies to arrive in early October; instead, the first ones appeared two weeks late, after an unusually warm September.$q$, $q$A semicolon before the conjunctive adverb "instead" correctly separates the two independent clauses, and the final phrase attaches with a comma. The version with a second semicolon is wrong because "after an unusually warm September" is a phrase, not an independent clause.$q$, 'draft', null)
