   $q$Read the sentence: "Students who practice coding frequently improve their problem-solving skills." The word "frequently" could describe either verb. Which revision makes clear that frequent practice is what matters?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Students who frequently practice coding improve their problem-solving skills.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Frequently, students who practice coding improve their problem-solving skills.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"modifier-attached-to-wrong-verb"},{"text":"Students who practice coding improve frequently their problem-solving skills.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"squinting-modifier-moved-wrong"},{"text":"Students who practice coding frequently, improve their problem-solving skills.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-between-subject-verb"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Students who frequently practice coding improve their problem-solving skills.$q$, $q$Placing "frequently" directly before "practice" ties it only to the practicing. Adding a comma after "frequently" separates the subject from its verb "improve," which is a punctuation error.$q$, 'draft', null)
