-- Passage wg11-writing-with-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('57329ddd-a95d-5346-b2ef-98b651da1a8b', 'wg11-writing-with-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: The Bell on the Last Ferry$q$, $q$(1) In Delphine Osei's short story "The Last Ferry to Calloway," sixteen-year-old Marisol spends a summer helping her grandfather run a small passenger ferry. (2) At first Marisol treats the job as a chore she must finish before she can see her friends. (3) Osei writes that Marisol "rang the departure bell the way someone slams a door, quick and without looking." (4) The comparison to a slammed door shows that Marisol wants the task over with and gives no thought to the passengers who depend on the signal.

(5) By the end of the story, the bell means something different to her. (6) When her grandfather's hands grow too stiff to steer, Marisol takes the wheel for the final crossing of the season. (7) Before leaving the dock, she "waited until the last runner from the bakery reached the ramp, then rang the bell twice, slowly, so the whole harbor could hear." (8) Ferries have carried passengers across rivers and bays for thousands of years. (9) The slow, careful ringing contrasts sharply with the hurried ringing at the start of the story. (10) This shows that Marisol changed.$q$, 'draft')
