-- Passage wg11-writing-with-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('552c5a16-ea51-552f-9486-690415890400', 'wg11-writing-with-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: Waiting Less, Riding More$q$, $q$(1) In her study "Waiting Less, Riding More," transportation researcher Fatima Haddad examines what happened after the city of Brennick installed arrival-time screens at 140 bus stops. (2) Haddad argues that the screens increased ridership mainly by making waiting feel shorter, even though buses did not actually arrive more often. (3) Her team found that the average wait stayed almost the same, about nine minutes, before and after the screens went up. (4) Yet in a rider survey, people at stops with screens estimated their wait at six minutes, while people at stops without screens guessed thirteen. (5) ____

(6) Haddad's ridership numbers point the same way. (7) Over two years, weekday boardings rose 11 percent at stops with screens and only 2 percent at stops without them. (8) One rider told the survey team that the screens were "the best thing the city has done in a decade." (9) Haddad notes that all 140 screens together cost the city less than a single new bus. (10) Still, Haddad admits that the stops chosen for screens were often busier to begin with, which could explain part of the difference in boardings.

(11) Overall, the screens seem pretty good.$q$, 'draft')
