   $q$Which sentence about the footbridge punctuates both modifiers correctly?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The newly built footbridge has a slip-resistant deck.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The newly-built footbridge has a slip resistant deck.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-hyphen-rules"},{"text":"The newly-built footbridge has a slip-resistant deck.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"hyphen-after-ly-adverb"},{"text":"The newly built footbridge has a slip resistant deck.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"missing-compound-hyphen"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The newly built footbridge has a slip-resistant deck.$q$, $q$An adverb ending in -ly, such as "newly," is never hyphenated to the word it modifies, but "slip-resistant" is a true compound adjective before "deck" and needs a hyphen. "Newly-built" is a common but incorrect habit.$q$, 'draft', null)
